Adult day services are daytime programs for older adults who need help with their day-to-day care. The services keep participants busy, healthy, and happy while they provide a regular opportunity to get out and socialize. They also give caregivers support and a sense of well-being to know that they can tend to their own needs while their loved one participates.
<a href="https://adultdayohio.org/" >AdultDayOhio.org</a> is a joint effort of the Ohio Department of Aging and LeadingAge Ohio to help Ohioans learn more about this affordable long-term care option.
&nbsp;

You must have a valid&nbsp;<a href="https://www.bmv.ohio.gov/driver-license.aspx" target="_blank"  aria-label="driver license, This link will open in a new window" rel="noopener">driver license</a> to operate a motor vehicle on Ohio's roads. An Ohio driver license is also a&nbsp;form of state-issued identification. With proper documents, your driver license can serve as a federally compliant&nbsp;<a href="https://www.bmv.ohio.gov/dl-real-id.aspx" target="_blank"  aria-label="Real ID, This link will open in a new window" rel="noopener">Real ID</a>&nbsp;for air travel and access to federal facilities.
If you are <a href="https://bmv.ohio.gov/new-to-ohio.aspx" >new to Ohio</a>, the Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles can help you understand your responsibility for transferring your driver license, vehicle title, and vehicle registration.
Ohio requires driving permits and supervised experience for anyone age 16-20 to get their license. The Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles help Ohioans who want to get their <a href="https://www.bmv.ohio.gov/dl-gdl.aspx" >first driver license</a> or learn more about <a href="https://www.bmv.ohio.gov/forms-general.aspx" >Ohio's driving laws</a>.&nbsp;
The Ohio Traffic Safety Office has <a href="https://otso.ohio.gov/programs/ohio-driver-training/for-drivers/parents-and-teens" >resources for new drivers and their families</a> as well as information about available <a href="https://otso.ohio.gov/programs/ohio-driver-training" target="_blank"  aria-label="driver training, This link will open in a new window" rel="noopener">driver training</a>. &nbsp;
The&nbsp;<a href="https://otso.ohio.gov/programs/ohio-driver-training/for-drivers/resources/road-ready-ohio" target="_blank"  aria-label="RoadReady Ohio, This link will open in a new window" rel="noopener">RoadReady Ohio</a>&nbsp;app uses cell phone technology to give real-time feedback and driving tips to new drivers. At the same time, it logs their required hours of supervised driving to get their driver license.&nbsp;
The Ohio Bureau of Motor Vehicles has information about different types of <a href="https://www.bmv.ohio.gov/suspensions-reinstatements.aspx" >driver license suspensions</a> and what drivers can do to safely and legally regain their driving privileges. They can also help you request your <a href="https://bmv.ohio.gov/more-record-types.aspx" >driver abstract and other records</a>.

The <a href="https://appgateway.drc.ohio.gov/OffenderSearch" >Offender (Inmate) Search</a> tool from the Ohio Department of Rehabilitation and Correction lets residents look up people currently serving time in an Ohio prison or under release.&nbsp;
To find someone in a county or city jail, contact your <a href="https://ohio.gov/government/resources/ohio-courts" >local court</a> or <a href="https://www.ohioattorneygeneral.gov/law-enforcement/law-enforcement-directory" >law enforcement agency</a>.
